Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||09-30-2000||10:33 AM||||Extreme: Yes I have seen that. When you jack the car up and 'unload' the suspension, you change the geomentry of the front end. When in proper alignment the wheels will "toe in" and also appear to point out to the sides in toward front (camber and caster) as the distance is now shorter at the tie rod ends. This is a result of the tires being lower as the springs now have no weight on them.<P>Contra,<P>If by chance you are referring to the 'bump stops' behind of the inner tie rod end, these act to protect the rack from metal to metal contact with the tie rod end and act as cushions when turning stop to stop (full left and right turning radius).<BR>I hope this is it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||09-30-2000||11:00 AM||||"bump steer" is the effect you have when you hit bumps at speed in your car - it makes the rear end kinda "fish-taily" -- best as I can explain it -- this is due to the toe-in/out of the rear wheels -- kinda makes the rear "squirrily".........an "anti-bump-steer" kit eliminates this from the Fiero. It is not that the Fiero was built with it but it is rather an effect due to the suspension set up of the '84 - '87 Fieros.<P>We (Fiero Shop, Richmond, CA - 1-510-233-4376) are soon coming out with a bump steer kit for the '84-'87 to eliminate this effect. It mainly involves a modification of the A-arms in the rear -- call us and we can explain more about it......<P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                        <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||10-03-2000||12:00 AM||||You'll notice that the pre-88 Fiero rear suspension is from the front of another car.  Pontiac engineers simply placed a strut rod (that's what I'll call it) in place of the tie-rod that would have been there if this set of wheels was to be able to steer.  When this suspension is mounted up front the tie-rod transmits steering wheel input to the wheels to turn them.  With this system mounted in back the strut rod takes the place of the tie-rod and holds the wheel straight (sort of).<P>The control arm pivots up and down in its bushings.  The strut rod is anchored lower (or is it higher) than the line drawn through the two control arm bushings.  The outer end of the strut rod is attached to the spindle behind the axel (this would actually be the steering input if this was mounted up front).  Now as the control arm rotates down this causes the strut rod to push out on the spindle.  Since the strut rod is attached behind the axel it pushes out on the spindle affactively turning the wheel to the left.  As the control arm rotates upward the strut rod pulls the wheel in effectively turning the wheel to the  right.<P>This is called Bump Steer because bumping the suspension up and down actually swings the wheel to the right and left.<P>This works much better with diagrams.  Oh well.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
